1
00:00:00,550 --> 00:00:06,850
Can we introduce this software? An employee wants an outcome, not another portal.

2
00:00:07,100 --> 00:00:13,450
Enterprise Skill Platform connects that request to the right capability, with governance built in.

3
00:00:14,950 --> 00:00:19,563
Different agents often repeat retrieval, checks, and reporting.

4
00:00:19,813 --> 00:00:32,175
ESP proposes a different unit of reuse: one governed skill, serving many experiences. Its contract keeps versions, permissions, and evidence requirements together.

5
00:00:33,683 --> 00:00:40,258
Follow the request: discover an eligible skill, check permissions, and invoke its approved plugin.

6
00:00:40,508 --> 00:00:48,833
Retain evidence before a decision. Discovery is not execution, and an automated check is not human approval.

7
00:00:50,350 --> 00:00:54,175
Here is a real record from the synthetic software review.

8
00:00:54,425 --> 00:01:04,787
The licensing finding links to an exact source excerpt and version. This is demonstration evidence, not a vendor certification or permission to install.

9
00:01:06,350 --> 00:01:10,887
The complete review retains a human decision and its limited scope.

10
00:01:11,138 --> 00:01:22,075
A separate missing-evidence review blocks approval. These are existing records, not a new approval made for this video. The development identity is shared.

11
00:01:23,617 --> 00:01:29,579
Two real calls demonstrate reuse: a standalone ticket lookup and a guidance workflow.

12
00:01:29,829 --> 00:01:37,892
Both use the same skill version and plugin operation. The workflow retains citations and linked audit records.

13
00:01:38,142 --> 00:01:43,767
Build once. Govern once. Reuse everywhere.
